---
title: Core concepts
description: Messages, handlers, the execution context and covariant matching on the stable v1 line.
---

Everything in Ergosfare is a **message** dispatched through a **pipeline** to one or more
**handlers**, with an **execution context** travelling alongside.

## Messages

Three shapes, one per module:

| Interface | Meaning | Handlers | Result |
|---|---|---|---|
| `ICommand`, `ICommand<TResult>` | do this | exactly one | optional |
| `IQuery<TResult>`, `IStreamQuery<TResult>` | tell me this | exactly one | always |
| `IEvent` | this happened | zero or more | none |

Declaring one means implementing a marker interface — no base class, nothing to override:

```csharp
public sealed record CreateProduct(string Name, decimal Price) : ICommand<Guid>;
public sealed record GetProduct(Guid Id) : IQuery<Product?>;
public sealed record ProductCreated(Guid Id, string Name) : IEvent;
```

Use `record` — commands and queries are data, and value equality plus `with` expressions are
what pre-interceptors need in order to rewrite a message. Use `sealed` unless you intend the
type to be a covariant base.

## Handlers

A handler implements the contract for its module and returns `Task`:

```csharp
public sealed class CreateProductHandler : ICommandHandler<CreateProduct, Guid>
{
    private readonly IProductStore _store;

    public CreateProductHandler(IProductStore store) => _store = store;

    public Task<Guid> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, IExecutionContext context)
        => _store.AddAsync(command.Name, command.Price, context.CancellationToken);
}
```

| Module | Contract |
|---|---|
| Commands | `ICommandHandler<TCommand>`, `ICommandHandler<TCommand, TResult>` |
| Queries | `IQueryHandler<TQuery, TResult>`, `IStreamQueryHandler<TQuery, TResult>` |
| Events | `IEventHandler<TEvent>` |

Commands and queries have **exactly one** main handler; registering two raises
`MultipleHandlerFoundException` at dispatch. Events broadcast to all of theirs.

Handlers resolve from the **calling DI scope** on every dispatch, so constructor injection
works normally and the lifetime is whatever you registered — singleton, scoped or transient.

{% aside type="note" %}
v2 moves this whole surface to `ValueTask`, so a synchronously completing handler allocates
nothing. See [the v2 migration guide](/ergosfare.docs/preview/migration/v1-to-v2).
{% /aside %}

## The execution context

`IExecutionContext` travels with a dispatch and carries the cancellation token, a key/value
bag scoped to that dispatch, and `Abort()`:

```csharp
public interface IExecutionContext
{
    CancellationToken CancellationToken { get; }
    IDictionary<object, object?> Items { get; }

    void Set(string key, object item);
    bool Has(string key);
    T Get<T>(string key) where T : notnull;
    bool TryGet<T>(string key, out T item);

    void Abort(object? messageResult = null);
}
```

A pre-interceptor can stash something the handler and later stages read back:

```csharp
context.Set("tenant", tenantId);
// …later
var tenant = context.Get<string>("tenant");
```

`Get<T>` throws when the key is absent or the type is wrong; use `TryGet<T>` when absence is
expected.

`Abort()` ends the pipeline immediately. If the message declares a result, supply one —
nothing downstream will:

```csharp
context.Abort(Guid.Empty);
```

### Ambient access

v1 also exposes the context statically through `AmbientExecutionContext`, backed by an
`AsyncLocal`:

```csharp
AmbientExecutionContext.IsEnabled = true;
var current = AmbientExecutionContext.Current;
```

{% aside type="caution" %}
`AmbientExecutionContext` is **deprecated since v1.2.0 and removed in v2**. Prefer the
`IExecutionContext` parameter that every handler and interceptor already receives — pass it
down to helpers rather than reaching for the ambient value. Code that avoids it now needs no
changes when you move to v2.
{% /aside %}

## Covariant matching

An interceptor registered against a base type or interface applies to **every assignable
message**, which is how one registration covers a family:

```csharp
public interface IAuditedCommand : ICommand;

public sealed record CreateProduct(string Name) : IAuditedCommand;
public sealed record DeleteProduct(Guid Id)     : IAuditedCommand;

public sealed class AuditTrail : ICommandPreInterceptor<IAuditedCommand> { … }
```

Within a stage, interceptors registered for the message's own type run **before** covariantly
matched ones, and `[Weight]` orders within each of those two groups (higher first).

On v1 this applies to **interceptors only**. Event *handlers* must name the exact event type;
covariant handler delivery arrived in
[v2](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/polymorphism).

## Modules

Commands, Queries and Events are independent modules over a shared core, each registered
separately:

```csharp
services.AddErgosfare(o => o
    .AddCommandModule(c => c.RegisterFromAssembly(assembly))
    .AddQueryModule(q => q.RegisterFromAssembly(assembly)));
```

Register only what you use. There is no single `IMediator`: a component takes
`ICommandMediator`, `IQueryMediator` or `IEventMediator`, and the type system keeps a
read-only component from sending commands.

Next: [Interceptors](/ergosfare.docs/interceptors).
